{"entries":{"chapter-78-study-peripheral-vascular-disease|Chapter 78 Study: Peripheral Vascular Disease":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|PAD affects up to 20% of people over 70 and commonly reflects atherosclerotic narrowing from the infrarenal aorta to tibial vessels (p1209).":["u3"],"copy|Vascular claudication occurs at a relatively reproducible walking distance and subsides with rest. Spinal stenosis symptoms have more variable onset and may improve with forward leaning (p1210).":["u4"],"copy|Examine distal lesions and pulses, but assess motor/sensory function when acute or severe ischemia is suspected; foot viability is not determined by pulses alone (p1210).":["u5"],"copy|ABI is the highest ankle systolic pressure divided by the highest brachial systolic pressure. Further imaging should fit the specialist's diagnostic/intervention plan (p1210–1211).":["u6"],"copy|Smoking cessation and supervised walking are central. Typical exercise sessions last 30 minutes, repeated three times weekly, walking as far beyond pain onset as safely tolerated (p1211).":["u7"],"copy|Antiplatelet treatment and a high-potency statin address vascular risk. Cilostazol may improve claudication distance; use caution with heart failure (p1211).":["u8"],"copy|Consider claudication intervention for lifestyle limitation after an exercise trial; progression to limb loss is not inevitable (p1209–1210).":["u9"],"copy|Carotid plaque can embolize and cause stroke; maximize antiplatelet, lipid and risk-factor management, including smoking cessation (p1212–1213).":["u10"],"copy|The chapter generally favors endarterectomy over stenting in older patients; stenting has specific indications and is not routinely indicated for asymptomatic older patients (p1209–1210).":["u11"],"copy|An abdominal aortic aneurysm is usually defined on ultrasound by an infrarenal aortic diameter ≥3.0 cm or an enlargement at least 50% above the expected diameter. It can remain silent until expansion or rupture (p1214, USPSTF AAA screening recommendation).":["u12"],"copy|For an unruptured AAA, ACC/AHA recommends repair at a maximal diameter ≥5.5 cm in men or ≥5.0 cm in women. Symptoms attributable to the aneurysm or rapid growth may warrant earlier repair. Endovascular repair can have short-term benefits, with later outcomes dependent on anatomy, follow-up and reintervention (p1210).":["u13"],"copy|Endovascular repair still requires long-term imaging follow-up; medical management includes smoking cessation and control of comorbid vascular risks (p1210, p1215).":["u14"],"copy|Chronic venous insufficiency results from venous hypertension, usually incompetent valves; elevation and compression are the mainstays (p1216–1217).":["u15"],"copy|AAA does not automatically prohibit aerobic activity: the chapter encourages activity and notes no rupture-prevention benefit demonstrated for doxycycline (p1215).":["u16"],"exam-questions|Exam questions":["u17"],"copy|2023-Jun Q8":["u18"],"copy|2024-May Q27":["u19"],"copy|CHAPTER 78s / HAZZARD":["u20"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 78 - Peripheral Vascular Disease; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u21"],"copy|Peripheral arterial disease":["u22"],"copy|Management & drugs":["u23"],"copy|Carotid disease & aneurysm":["u24"],"copy|Venous disease & exam traps":["u25"],"copy|Past-exam questions linked to this chapter (2):":["u26"],"copy|Antiplatelet treatment and a high-potency statin address vascular risk. Cilostazol may improve claudication distance and is contraindicated in heart failure of any severity (p1211 and 2024 ACC/AHA PAD guideline, section 5.7).":["u8"],"copy|The following subspecialty questions cite eighth-edition pages in this chapter.":["u26"],"copy|Antiplatelet therapy and statins address vascular risk. Hazzard describes improved walking distance with cilostazol and advises caution in heart failure (p1211). Current practice (not the book): Cilostazol is contraindicated in heart failure of any severity. ACC/AHA 2024, §5.7.":["u8"],"copy|Hazzard defines AAA as enlargement of vessel diameter by more than 50%, generally over 3 cm in men (p1214). Current practice (not the book): Screening guidance commonly defines AAA as an aortic diameter of 3.0 cm or greater. USPSTF, 2019.":["u207"],"copy|For a good surgical candidate the chapter gives diameter >5.5 cm in men or >5 cm in women or rapid growth >0.50 cm in six months as indications for intervention (p1215). Endovascular repair can have short-term benefits, with later outcomes dependent on anatomy, follow-up and reintervention (pp1215–1216).":["u205"],"copy|The book calls it a phosphodiesterase II inhibitor [sic — the drug inhibits phosphodiesterase III] and advises caution in heart failure (p1211).":["u59"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): Cilostazol is a PDE-III inhibitor and must not be given in heart failure of any severity.":["u208"],"copy|The book recommends intervention in symptomatic patients with more than 70% stenosis (p1213).":["u209"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): Endarterectomy can be appropriate in selected patients with symptomatic 50%–69% narrowing.":["u92"],"copy|The recommendation is stronger at 70%–99%.":["u93"],"copy|The chapter recommends intervention for >70% symptomatic or >80% asymptomatic stenosis in centers with excellent outcomes and patients with anticipated survival exceeding five years.":["u101"],"copy|The book also lists significant coronary artery disease and congestive heart failure among reasons to consider carotid stenting.":["u109"],"copy|Hazzard gives more than 80% as its asymptomatic threshold on p1213 and describes greater than 70%–80% on p1214.":["u210"],"copy|Consider anticipated survival and procedural risk.":["u211"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): CREST-2 subsequently studied high-grade stenosis without recent symptoms in two parallel trials at experienced centers.":["u116"],"copy|The trials did not randomize patients between the two procedures.":["u120"],"copy|Table 78-2 (p1216) presents weak SVS recommendations based on low-quality evidence and lists three years for 3.0–3.9 cm, one year for 4.0–4.9 cm and six months for 5.0–5.4 cm.":["u166"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): ACC/AHA starts six-monthly surveillance at 5.0 cm in men and 4.5 cm in women.":["u212"],"copy|Hazzard considers elective repair in good surgical candidates with diameters greater than 5.5 cm in men or 5 cm in women.":["u175"],"copy|Expansion greater than 0.50 cm in six months can also indicate intervention.":["u176"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): ACC/AHA recommends repair at ≥5.5 cm in men or ≥5.0 cm in women, or for attributable symptoms; growth ≥0.5 cm in six months may justify repair.":["u213"]},"maxId":213,"sequenceOffsets":[[214,-187],[226,-20],[227,-188],[247,-187],[279,-183],[284,-182],[291,-181],[295,-180],[296,-179],[299,-177],[343,-176],[344,-175],[350,-173],[378,0]]}
